WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.400
Who owns the boundary?

2
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.612
The rule is one decider per row.

3
00:00:03.612 --> 00:00:07.991
The authorizing official determines
the boundary, signs every exception,

4
00:00:07.991 --> 00:00:11.099
and decides whether a change
needs reauthorization.

5
00:00:11.099 --> 00:00:13.517
That risk acceptance can't be delegated,

6
00:00:13.517 --> 00:00:17.325
and in federal agencies it's held
by government personnel only.

7
00:00:17.325 --> 00:00:19.750
The system owner is the boundary owner.

8
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.580
They keep the boundary
record and the package,

9
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.112
and they decide where a new workload goes.

10
00:00:25.112 --> 00:00:29.022
That matters, because vending an
unrecorded subscription is the

11
00:00:29.022 --> 00:00:33.076
cheapest way to turn an authorized
system into an unauthorized one.

12
00:00:33.076 --> 00:00:35.508
The CISO sets control requirements,

13
00:00:35.508 --> 00:00:39.588
and the ISSO reads provider change
notices against the record.

14
00:00:39.588 --> 00:00:44.837
The platform team builds everything, but
doesn't decide whether a gap is acceptable.

15
00:00:44.837 --> 00:00:47.495
And the contract is a
boundary decision too:

16
00:00:47.495 --> 00:00:51.112
only a contracting officer
changes what the agency bought.

17
00:00:51.112 --> 00:00:53.210
Next, handling what's outside.
